Now the symlink test failure in wasm_shell_host_cwd_test. It failed with args `['/tmp/fah_1274NYVCSX/link/t.txt']` — meaning the cwd didn't map. Why? The shell's `SandboxHostRoot` resolves `_root` = sandbox.path... wait: `io.Directory(_root).resolveSymbolicLinksSync()` resolves `/tmp/fah_1274XXX/var/mobile/...` — but `/tmp` itself may be a symlink! On this runner /tmp might be fine. The cwd is `${tmp.path}/link` where link → sandbox.path. resolved root = resolveSymbolicLinks(sandbox.path). If /tmp is real, resolved root == _root, so candidates has only _root, and pathKey = link path doesn't match. The failure shows cwd passed through as-is.

So resolution didn't help because the resolved root equals the as-given root — the symlink is in the *cwd*, not the root. To match a symlinked cwd, we'd need to resolve the cwd too (per-call), or the helper should resolve the *path* as well. The reviewer suggested "compare against resolveSymbolicLinksSync()" — ambiguous which side. Resolving the cwd per call: `io.Directory(trimmed).resolveSymbolicLinksSync()` — cwd typically exists (env cwd is a real dir). Cost: one realpath syscall per exec — acceptable, but failures (nonexistent) must fall back.

But careful: stripToSandboxPath is also used by `_hostPath` and `_map` on arbitrary file paths (which may not exist — e.g. a path about to be created). Resolving a nonexistent path throws → catch → use as-is.

Hmm, but resolving arbitrary paths in `_map` for every file op adds syscalls per op. Trade-off. The main threat is cwd; file operands rarely pass through _map as host paths... Actually _map receives host paths derived from env.cwd joins — those exist usually.

Alternative approach: resolve both the path and the root when the direct match fails, i.e. lazy: try direct match first (cheap), and only if it fails, attempt resolveSymbolicLinksSync on the path (one extra syscall on miss) and retry. Misses are rare. That keeps the common path cheap and fixes symlinked cwd.

Also note: resolving the *parent* — realpath on a nonexistent path fails entirely, even if only the last component doesn't exist. For cwd that's fine (exists). For _map paths that don't exist → catch → null. OK.

Let me update `stripToSandboxPath`:

```dart
  String? stripToSandboxPath(String path) {
    if (path.isEmpty) return null;
    final trimmed = _stripTrailingSeparator(path);
    final hit = _stripMatched(trimmed);
    if (hit != null) return hit;
    // Symlink spelling variants (/var → /private/var on the iOS
    // simulator): resolve the path itself and retry. The path may not
    // exist (e.g. a file about to be created) — resolution failure just
    // means no variant match.
    String? resolved;
    try {
      resolved = io.Directory(trimmed).resolveSymbolicLinksSync();
    } on Object {
      return null;
    }
    if (resolved == trimmed) return null;
    return _stripMatched(_stripTrailingSeparator(resolved));
  }
```

with `_stripMatched` as the loop. And `_resolvedRoot` also kept (root stored through a symlink). Both.

Edge: case-fold + resolved: handled by _stripMatched.

One concern: performance — `_effectiveCwd` called ~1-3 times per exec; on direct hit no extra syscall. On miss (guest paths like '/work'), an extra failed realpath attempt... `resolveSymbolicLinksSync` on nonexistent path throws — cost is one syscall, fine.

Wait — but for guest paths that DON'T exist as host paths (e.g. '/work' relative to process cwd — io.Directory('/work').resolveSymbolicLinksSync() → throws since /work doesn't exist on host... actually on the host, '/work' refers to host root! Danger: guest path '/work' → resolveSymbolicLinks('/work') — if host has /work dir, it resolves to '/work' — same string → no match → null. OK fine. If host doesn't have /work → throws → null. No harm: we only *match*, never rewrite unless under root. The returned value is always derived from the matched root prefix. Safe.

But hold on — could resolving introduce a false match? resolved host path under the sandbox root: path '/x' where host resolves to '/x'? Then trimmed '/x' == root → would have matched directly already. Fine.

Update the helper.

{"newText":"  /// The sandbox-absolute remainder of [path] under the root: `/` when
  /// [path] *is* the root, `/work/...` when it sits under it (lexically
  /// normalized); `null` when [path] is outside the root.
  String? stripToSandboxPath(String path) {
    if (path.isEmpty) return null;
    final trimmed = _stripTrailingSeparator(path);
    final direct = _stripMatched(trimmed);
    if (direct != null) return direct;
    // Symlink spelling variant (/var → /private/var on the iOS simulator):
    // the symlink can sit in the path itself, not just the root, so
    // resolve the path and retry. The path may not exist (e.g. a file
    // about to be created) — a resolution failure simply means no match.
    String resolved;
    try {
      resolved = io.Directory(trimmed).resolveSymbolicLinksSync();
    } on Object {
      return null;
    }
    if (resolved == trimmed) return null;
    return _stripMatched(_stripTrailingSeparator(resolved));
  }

  String? _stripMatched(String trimmed) {
    final pathKey = _key(trimmed);
    final candidates = <String>[_root];
    final resolved = _resolvedRoot;
    if (resolved != null && resolved != _root) candidates.add(resolved);
    for (final root in candidates) {
      final rootKey = _key(root);
      if (pathKey == rootKey) return '/';
      final prefix = '$rootKey/';
      if (pathKey.startsWith(prefix)) {
        // Cut in the *original* string: case folding can shift lengths,
        // so the folded prefix length is not safe to use as an index.
        final cut = trimmed.length - (pathKey.length - prefix.length);
        return normalizeLexicalPath(trimmed.substring(cut));
      }
    }
    return null;
  }","oldText":"  /// The sandbox-absolute remainder of [path] under the root: `/` when
